    <statement><p>Calculate the length of the path over the given interval.
<me>c(t) = (3 t^2, 4 t^3), \, 1 \le t \le 3</me></p>
<p><fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{2\cdot 6}{3\cdot 8}\mathopen{}\left(37^{\frac{3}{2}}-5^{\frac{3}{2}}\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x = 3 t^2</m> and <m>y = 4 t^3</m>.  Hence <m>x' = 6 t</m> and <m>y' = 12 t^2</m>.  By the formula for the arc length we get
<me>S = \int ^{3} _{1} \sqrt{x'(t)^2 + y'(t)^2} \, dt = \int ^{3} _{1} \sqrt{36 t^2 + 144 t^4} \, dt = 6 \int ^{3} _{1} \sqrt{1 + 4 t^2} \, t \, dt</me>
Using the substitution <m>u = 1 + 4 t^2, \, du = 8 t \, dt</m> we obtain
<me>S = \frac{6}{8} \int ^{37} _{5} \sqrt{u} \, du = \frac{6}{8} \cdot \frac{2}{3} u^{3/2} \mid ^{37} _{5} = \frac{12}{24} ((37)^{3/2} - (5)^{3/2}) = 106.941</me>


</solution>
